\fs32 \cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa127\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 Mojo requires a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/requirements/#gpu-compatibility"}}{\fldrslt \ul compatible GPU development environment}} to compile kernel functions, otherwise it raises a compile-time error. In our code, we're using the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/metaprogramming/comptime-evaluation/#comptime-if"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cb20 \ul comptime if}} statement to evaluate the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 has_accelerator()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  function at compile time and compile only the corresponding branch of the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 if
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  statement. As a result, if you don't have a compatible GPU development environment, you'll see the following message when you run the program:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf16 \cb20 No compatible GPU found\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 In that case, you need to find a system that has a supported GPU to continue with this tutorial.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 3. Define a simple kernel\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A GPU kernel is simply a function that runs on a GPU, executing a specific computation on a large dataset in parallel across thousands or millions of threads. You might already be familiar with threads when programming for a CPU, but GPU threads are different. On a CPU, threads are managed by the operating system and can perform completely independent tasks, such as managing a user interface, fetching data from a database, and so on. But on a GPU, threads are managed by the GPU itself. All the threads on a GPU execute the same kernel function, but they each work on a different part of the data.\
When you run a kernel, you need to specify the number of threads you want to use. The number of threads you specify depends on the size of the data you want to process and the amount of parallelism you want to achieve. A common strategy is to use one thread per element of data in the result. So if you're performing an element-wise addition of two 1,024-element vectors, you'd use 1,024 threads.\
A grid is the top-level organizational structure for the threads executing a kernel function. A grid consists of multiple thread blocks, which are further divided into individual threads that execute the kernel function concurrently. The GPU assigns a unique block index to each thread block, and a unique thread index to each thread within a block. Threads within the same thread block can share data through shared memory and synchronize using built-in mechanisms, but they cannot directly communicate with threads in other blocks. For this tutorial, we won't get into the details of why or how to do this, but it's an important concept to keep in mind when you're writing more complex kernels.\
To better understand how grids, thread blocks, and threads are organized, let's write a simple kernel function that prints the thread block and thread indices. Add the following code to your 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 vector_addition.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  file:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf16 \cb22 vector_addition.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 from\cf16  std\cf5 .\cf16 gpu \cf5 import\cf16  block_idx\cf5 ,\cf16  thread_idx\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 def\cf16  \cf14 print_threads\cf5 ():\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf23 """Print thread IDs."""\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 print(\cf23 "Block index: ["\cf5 ,\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         block_idx\cf5 .\cf16 x\cf5 ,\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf23 "]\\tThread index: ["\cf5 ,\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         thread_idx\cf5 .\cf16 x\cf5 ,\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf23 "]"\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 4. Compile and run the kernel\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Next, we need to update the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 main()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function to compile the kernel function for our GPU and then run it, specifying the number of thread blocks in the grid and the number of threads per thread block. For this initial example, let's define a grid consisting of 2 thread blocks, each with 64 threads.\
Modify the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 main()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function and add 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 has_apple_gpu_accelerator()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to the list of imports from the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 sys
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  module so that your program looks like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf16 \cb22 vector_addition.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 from\cf16  std\cf5 .\cf16 sys \cf5 import\cf16  has_accelerator\cf5 ,\cf16  has_apple_gpu_accelerator\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 from\cf16  std\cf5 .\cf16 gpu\cf5 .\cf16 host \cf5 import\cf16  DeviceContext\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 from\cf16  std\cf5 .\cf16 gpu \cf5 import\cf16  block_idx\cf5 ,\cf16  thread_idx\cb1 \
\
\
\cf5 \cb15 def\cf16  \cf14 print_threads\cf5 ():\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf23 """Print thread IDs."""\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 print(\cf23 "Block index: ["\cf5 ,\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         block_idx\cf5 .\cf16 x\cf5 ,\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf23 "]\\tThread index: ["\cf5 ,\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         thread_idx\cf5 .\cf16 x\cf5 ,\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf23 "]"\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\
\cf5 \cb15 def\cf16  \cf14 main\cf5 ()\cf16  raises\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 comptime\cf16  \cf5 if\cf16  \cf5 not\cf16  has_accelerator\cf5 ():\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 print(\cf23 "No compatible GPU found"\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 elif\cf16  has_apple_gpu_accelerator\cf5 ():\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 print(\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             \cf23 "Printing from a kernel is not currently supported on Apple silicon"\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             \cf23 " GPUs"\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 else:\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         ctx \cf5 =\cf16  DeviceContext\cf5 ()\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         ctx\cf5 .\cf16 enqueue_function\cf5 [\cf16 print_threads\cf5 ,\cf16  print_threads\cf5 ](\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             grid_dim\cf5 =\cf17 2\cf5 ,\cf16  block_dim\cf5 =\cf17 64\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         ctx\cf5 .\cf16 synchronize\cf5 ()\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 print(\cf23 "Program finished"\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

