\fs28 \cf24 \cb25 If you're completing this tutorial on macOS\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa127\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb25 Printing from within a kernel function is not currently supported on Apple silicon GPUs. If you run this program on macOS, you'll see only the following output:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf16 \cb20 Printing from a kernel is not currently supported on Apple silicon GPUs\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb25 If you're completing this tutorial on macOS, go ahead and read the rest of this step and the next step to learn the concepts of compiling and running a kernel. You'll be able to continue the hands-on portion of the tutorial starting with \ul 6. Allocate host memory for the input vectors\ulnone , where we'll begin writing a different kernel that doesn't use 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 print()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb25 .\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Typical CPU-GPU interaction is asynchronous, allowing the GPU to process tasks while the CPU is busy with other work. Each 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 DeviceContext
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  has an associated stream of queued operations to execute on the GPU. Operations within a stream execute in the order they are issued.\
The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/device_context/DeviceContext/#enqueue_function"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 \ul \ulc5 enqueue_function()}} method compiles a kernel function and enqueues it to run on the given device. You must provide the name of the kernel function as a compile-time Mojo parameter, and the following arguments:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Any additional arguments specified by the kernel function definition (none, in this case).\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The grid dimensions using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 grid_dim
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword argument.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The thread block dimensions using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 block_dim
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword argument.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 (See the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/functions/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Functions}} section of the Mojo Manual for more information on Mojo function arguments and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/parameters/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Parameters}} section for more information on Mojo compile-time parameters and metaprogramming.)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 API variants\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa127\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb12 The 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 compile_function_unchecked()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  and 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 enqueue_function_unchecked()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  methods are deprecated and will be removed in a future release because they don't perform compile-time type checking of the kernel function arguments. You'll encounter run-time errors if the values you pass don't conform to the expected argument types.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb12 The 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 compile_function_experimental()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  and 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 enqueue_function_experimental()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  methods perform compile-time type checking but require the kernel function as a parameter only once instead of twice. Once we verify these APIs work correctly, we'll rename them to 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 compile_function()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  and 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 enqueue_function()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  to make type-checking the default behavior.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In this example, we're invoking the compiled kernel function with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 grid_dim=2
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 block_dim=64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , which means we're using a grid of 2 thread blocks with 64 threads each, for a total of 128 threads in the grid.\
When you run a kernel, the GPU assigns each thread block within the grid to a streaming multiprocessor for execution. A streaming multiprocessor (SM) is the fundamental processing unit of a GPU, designed to execute multiple parallel workloads efficiently. Each SM contains several cores, which perform the actual computations of the threads executing on the SM, along with shared resources like registers, shared memory, and control mechanisms to coordinate the execution of threads. The number of SMs and the number of cores on a GPU depends on its architecture. For example, the NVIDIA H100 PCIe contains 114 SMs, with 128 32-bit floating point cores per SM.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 {{\NeXTGraphic Attachment.png \width320 \height320 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs27\fsmilli13600 \cf2 Figure 1. High-level architecture of a streaming multiprocessor (SM). (Click to enlarge.)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Additionally, when an SM is assigned a thread block, it divides the block into multiple warps, which are groups of 32 or 64 threads, depending on the GPU architecture. These threads execute the same instruction simultaneously in a single instruction, multiple threads (SIMT) model. The SM's warp scheduler coordinates the execution of warps on the SM's cores.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 {{\NeXTGraphic Attachment.png \width320 \height320 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs27\fsmilli13600 \cf2 Figure 2. Hierarchy of threads running on a GPU, showing the relationship of the grid, thread blocks, warps, and individual threads, based on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://rocm.docs.amd.com/projects/HIP/en/latest/understand/programming_model.html"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 HIP Programming Guide}}\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Warps are used to efficiently utilize GPU hardware by maximizing throughput and minimizing control overhead. Since GPUs are designed for high-performance parallel processing, grouping threads into warps allows for streamlined instruction scheduling and execution, reducing the complexity of managing individual threads. Multiple warps from multiple thread blocks can be active within an SM at any given time, enabling the GPU to keep execution units busy. For example, if the threads of a particular warp are blocked waiting for data from memory, the warp scheduler can immediately switch execution to another warp that's ready to run.\
After enqueuing the kernel function, we want to ensure that the CPU waits for it to finish execution before exiting the program. We do this by calling the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/device_context/DeviceContext/#synchronize"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 \ul \ulc5 synchronize()}} method of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 DeviceContext
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  object, which blocks until the device completes all operations in its queue.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 5. Manage grid dimensions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The grid in the previous step consisted of a one-dimensional grid of 2 thread blocks with 64 threads in each block. However, you can also organize the thread blocks in a two- or three-dimensional grid. Similarly, you can arrange the threads in a thread block across one, two, or three dimensions. Typically, you determine the dimensions of the grid and thread blocks based on the dimensionality of the data to process. For example, you might choose a 1-dimensional grid for processing large vectors, a 2-dimensional grid for processing matrices, and a 3-dimensional grid for processing the frames of a video.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 {{\NeXTGraphic Attachment.png \width320 \height320 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs27\fsmilli13600 \cf2 Figure 3. Organization of thread blocks and threads within a grid.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To better understand how grids, thread blocks, and threads work together, let's modify our 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 print_threads()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  kernel function to print the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 x
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 y
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 z
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  components of the thread block and thread indices for each thread.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf16 \cb22 vector_addition.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 def\cf16  \cf14 print_threads\cf5 ():\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf23 """Print thread IDs."""\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 print(\cf23 "Block index: ["\cf5 ,\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         block_idx\cf5 .\cf16 x\cf5 ,\cf16  block_idx\cf5 .\cf16 y\cf5 ,\cf16  block_idx\cf5 .\cf16 z\cf5 ,\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf23 "]\\tThread index: ["\cf5 ,\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         thread_idx\cf5 .\cf16 x\cf5 ,\cf16  thread_idx\cf5 .\cf16 y\cf5 ,\cf16  thread_idx\cf5 .\cf16 z\cf5 ,\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf23 "]"\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Then, update 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 main()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to enqueue the kernel function with a 2x2x1 grid of thread blocks and a 16x4x2 arrangement of threads within each thread block:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf16 \cb22 vector_addition.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

