\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 The maximum number of threads per thread block and threads per SM is GPU-specific. For example, the NVIDIA A100 GPU has a maximum of 1,024 threads per thread block and 2,048 threads per SM.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb12 Choosing the size and shape of the grid and thread blocks is a balancing act between maximizing the number of threads that can execute concurrently and minimizing the amount of time spent waiting for data to be loaded from memory. Factors such as the size of the data to process, the number of SMs on the GPU, and the memory bandwidth of the GPU can all play a role in determining the optimal grid and thread block dimensions. One general guideline is to choose a thread block size that is a multiple of the warp size. This helps to maximize the utilization of the GPU's resources and minimizes the overhead of managing multiple warps.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Now that you understand how to manage grid dimensions, let's get ready to create a kernel that performs a simple element-wise addition of two vectors of floating point numbers.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 6. Allocate host memory for the input vectors\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Before creating the two input vectors for our kernel function, we need to understand the distinction between host memory and device memory. Host memory is dynamic random-access memory (DRAM) accessible by the CPU, whereas device memory is DRAM accessible by the GPU. If you have data in host memory, you must explicitly copy it to device memory before you can use it in a kernel function. Similarly, if your kernel function produces data that you want the CPU to use later, you must explicitly copy it back to host memory.\
For this tutorial, we'll use the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/device_context/HostBuffer/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 \ul \ulc5 HostBuffer}} type to represent our vectors on the host. A 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 HostBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is a block of host memory associated with a particular 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 DeviceContext
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . It supports methods for transferring data between host and device memory, as well as a basic set of methods for accessing data elements by index and for printing the buffer.\
Let's update 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 main()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to create two 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 HostBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 s for our input vectors and initialize them with values. You won't need the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 print_threads()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  kernel function anymore, so you can remove it and the code to compile and invoke it.\
After making these changes, your 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 vector_addition.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  file should look like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf16 \cb22 vector_addition.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 from\cf16  std\cf5 .\cf16 gpu\cf5 .\cf16 host \cf5 import\cf16  DeviceContext\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 from\cf16  std\cf5 .\cf16 gpu \cf5 import\cf16  block_idx\cf5 ,\cf16  thread_idx\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 from\cf16  std\cf5 .\cf16 sys \cf5 import\cf16  has_accelerator\cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb15 # Vector data type and size\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb15 comptime\cf16  float_dtype \cf5 =\cf16  DType\cf5 .\cf16 float32\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 comptime\cf16  vector_size \cf5 =\cf16  \cf17 1000\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\
\cf5 \cb15 def\cf16  \cf14 main\cf5 ()\cf16  raises\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 comptime\cf16  \cf5 if\cf16  \cf5 not\cf16  has_accelerator\cf5 ():\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 print(\cf23 "No compatible GPU found"\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 else:\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf6 # Get the context for the attached GPU\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         ctx \cf5 =\cf16  DeviceContext\cf5 ()\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15         \cf6 # Create HostBuffers for input vectors\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         lhs_host_buffer \cf5 =\cf16  ctx\cf5 .\cf16 enqueue_create_host_buffer\cf5 [\cf16 float_dtype\cf5 ](\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             vector_size\cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         rhs_host_buffer \cf5 =\cf16  ctx\cf5 .\cf16 enqueue_create_host_buffer\cf5 [\cf16 float_dtype\cf5 ](\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             vector_size\cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         ctx\cf5 .\cf16 synchronize\cf5 ()\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15         \cf6 # Initialize the input vectors\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 for\cf16  i \cf5 in\cf16  \cf14 range\cf5 (\cf16 vector_size\cf5 ):\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             lhs_host_buffer\cf5 [\cf16 i\cf5 ]\cf16  \cf5 =\cf16  Float32\cf5 (\cf16 i\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             rhs_host_buffer\cf5 [\cf16 i\cf5 ]\cf16  \cf5 =\cf16  Float32\cf5 (\cf16 Float64\cf5 (\cf16 i\cf5 )\cf16  \cf5 *\cf16  \cf17 0.5\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15         \cf5 print(\cf23 "LHS buffer: "\cf5 ,\cf16  lhs_host_buffer\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 print(\cf23 "RHS buffer: "\cf5 ,\cf16  rhs_host_buffer\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/device_context/DeviceContext/#enqueue_create_host_buffer"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 \ul \ulc5 enqueue_create_host_buffer()}} method accepts the data type as a compile-time parameter and the size of the buffer as a run-time argument and returns a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 HostBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . As with all 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 DeviceContext
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  methods whose name starts with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 enqueue_
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the method is asynchronous and returns immediately, adding the operation to the queue to be executed by the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 DeviceContext
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Therefore, we need to call the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 synchronize()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method to ensure that the operation has completed before we use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 HostBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  object. Then we can initialize the input vectors with values and print them.\
Now let's run the program to verify that everything is working so far.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 mojo vector_addition.mojo\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You should see the following output:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 LHS buffer:  HostBuffer([0.0, 1.0, 2.0, ..., 997.0, 998.0, 999.0])\cb1 \
\cb15 RHS buffer:  HostBuffer([0.0, 0.5, 1.0, ..., 498.5, 499.0, 499.5])\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 You might notice that we don't explicitly call any methods to free the host memory allocated by our 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 HostBuffer
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 s. That's because a 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 HostBuffer
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  is subject to Mojo's standard ownership and lifecycle mechanisms. The Mojo compiler analyzes our program to determine the last point that the owner of or a reference to an object is used and automatically adds a call to the object's destructor. In our program, we last reference the buffers at the end of our program's 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 main()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  method. However, in a more complex program, the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 HostBuffer
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  could persist across calls to multiple kernel functions if it is referenced at later points in the program. See the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/ownership/"}}{\fldrslt \ul Ownership}} and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/"}}{\fldrslt \ul Intro to value lifecycle}} sections of the Mojo Manual for more information on Mojo value ownership and value lifecycle management.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 7. Copy the input vectors to GPU memory and allocate an output vector\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Now that we have our input vectors allocated and initialized on the CPU, let's copy them to the GPU so they'll be available for the kernel function to use. While we're at it, we'll also allocate memory on the GPU for the output vector that will hold the result of the kernel function.\
Add the following code to the end of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 main()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf16 \cb22 vector_addition.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15         \cf6 # Create DeviceBuffers for the input vectors\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         lhs_device_buffer \cf5 =\cf16  ctx\cf5 .\cf16 enqueue_create_buffer\cf5 [\cf16 float_dtype\cf5 ](\cf16 vector_size\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         rhs_device_buffer \cf5 =\cf16  ctx\cf5 .\cf16 enqueue_create_buffer\cf5 [\cf16 float_dtype\cf5 ](\cf16 vector_size\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15         \cf6 # Copy the input vectors from the HostBuffers to the DeviceBuffers\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         ctx\cf5 .\cf16 enqueue_copy\cf5 (\cf16 dst_buf\cf5 =\cf16 lhs_device_buffer\cf5 ,\cf16  src_buf\cf5 =\cf16 lhs_host_buffer\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         ctx\cf5 .\cf16 enqueue_copy\cf5 (\cf16 dst_buf\cf5 =\cf16 rhs_device_buffer\cf5 ,\cf16  src_buf\cf5 =\cf16 rhs_host_buffer\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15         \cf6 # Create a DeviceBuffer for the result vector\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         result_device_buffer \cf5 =\cf16  ctx\cf5 .\cf16 enqueue_create_buffer\cf5 [\cf16 float_dtype\cf5 ](\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             vector_size\cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/device_context/DeviceBuffer/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 \ul \ulc5 DeviceBuffer}} type is analogous to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 HostBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type, but represents a block of device memory associated with a particular 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 DeviceContext
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Specifically, the buffer is located in the device's global memory space, which is accessible by all threads executing on the device. As with a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 HostBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 DeviceBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is subject to Mojo's standard ownership and lifecycle mechanisms. It persists until it is no longer referenced in the program or until the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 DeviceContext
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  itself is destroyed.\
The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/device_context/DeviceContext/#enqueue_create_buffer"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 \ul \ulc5 enqueue_create_buffer()}} method accepts the data type as a compile-time parameter and the size of the buffer as a run-time argument and returns a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 DeviceBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . The operation is asynchronous, but we don't need to call the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 synchronize()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method yet because we have more operations to add to the queue.\
The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/device_context/DeviceContext/#enqueue_copy"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 \ul \ulc5 enqueue_copy()}} method is overloaded to support copying from host to device, device to host, or even device to device for systems that have multiple GPUs. In this example, we use it to copy the data in our 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 HostBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 s to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 DeviceBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 s.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa127\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 Both 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 DeviceBuffer
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  and 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 HostBuffer
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  also include {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/device_context/DeviceBuffer/#enqueue_copy_to"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cb20 \ul enqueue_copy_to()}} and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/device_context/DeviceBuffer/#enqueue_copy_from"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cb20 \ul enqueue_copy_from()}} methods. These are simply convenience methods that call the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 enqueue_copy()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  method on their corresponding 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 DeviceContext
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 . Therefore, we could have written the copy operations in the previous example with the following equivalent code:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf16 \cb20     lhs_host_buffer\cf5 .\cf16 enqueue_copy_to\cf5 (\cf16 dst\cf5 =\cf16 lhs_device_buffer\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb20     rhs_host_buffer\cf5 .\cf16 enqueue_copy_to\cf5 (\cf16 dst\cf5 =\cf16 rhs_device_buffer\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 8. Create 
\fs56 \cf10 \cb13 LayoutTensor
\fs64 \cf2 \cb1  views\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 One last step before writing the kernel function is that we're going to create a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/kernels/layout/layout_tensor/LayoutTensor/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 \ul \ulc5 LayoutTensor}} view for each of the vectors. 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 LayoutTensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  provides a powerful abstraction for multi-dimensional data with precise control over memory organization. It supports various memory layouts (row-major, column-major, tiled), hardware-specific optimizations, and efficient parallel access patterns. We don't need all of these features for this tutorial, but in more complex kernels, it's a useful tool for manipulating data. Even though it isn't strictly necessary for this example, we'll use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 LayoutTensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  so that you can get familiar with it.\
First, add the following import to the top of the file:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf16 \cb22 vector_addition.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 from\cf16  layout \cf5 import\cf16  Layout\cf5 ,\cf16  LayoutTensor\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/kernels/layout/layout/Layout/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 \ul \ulc5 Layout}} is a representation of memory layouts using shape and stride information, and it maps between logical coordinates and linear memory indices. We'll need to use the same 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Layout
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  definition multiple times, so add the following 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value to the top of the file after the other 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  declarations:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf16 \cb22 vector_addition.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 comptime\cf16  layout \cf5 =\cf16  Layout\cf5 .\cf16 row_major\cf5 (\cf16 vector_size\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Finally, add the following code to the end of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 main()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function to create 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 LayoutTensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  views for each of the vectors:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf16 \cb22 vector_addition.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15         \cf6 # Wrap the DeviceBuffers in LayoutTensors\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         lhs_tensor \cf5 =\cf16  LayoutTensor\cf5 [\cf16 float_dtype\cf5 ,\cf16  layout\cf5 ](\cf16 lhs_device_buffer\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         rhs_tensor \cf5 =\cf16  LayoutTensor\cf5 [\cf16 float_dtype\cf5 ,\cf16  layout\cf5 ](\cf16 rhs_device_buffer\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         result_tensor \cf5 =\cf16  LayoutTensor\cf5 [\cf16 float_dtype\cf5 ,\cf16  layout\cf5 ](\cf16 result_device_buffer\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
