\fs64 \cf2 9. Define the vector addition kernel function\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Now we're ready to write the kernel function. First, add the following imports (note that we've added 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 block_dim
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to the list of imports from 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 gpu
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ):\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf16 \cb22 vector_addition.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 from\cf16  std\cf5 .\cf16 gpu \cf5 import\cf16  block_dim\cf5 ,\cf16  block_idx\cf5 ,\cf16  thread_idx\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 from\cf16  std\cf5 .\cf16 math \cf5 import\cf16  ceildiv\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Then, add the following code to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 vector_addition.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  just before the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 main()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf16 \cb22 vector_addition.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb15 # Calculate the number of thread blocks needed by dividing the vector size\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf6 \cb15 # by the block size and rounding up.\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb15 comptime\cf16  block_size \cf5 =\cf16  \cf17 256\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 comptime\cf16  num_blocks \cf5 =\cf16  ceildiv\cf5 (\cf16 vector_size\cf5 ,\cf16  block_size\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\
\cf5 \cb15 def\cf16  \cf14 vector_addition\cf5 (\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     lhs_tensor\cf5 :\cf16  LayoutTensor\cf5 [\cf16 float_dtype\cf5 ,\cf16  layout\cf5 ,\cf16  MutAnyOrigin\cf5 ],\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     rhs_tensor\cf5 :\cf16  LayoutTensor\cf5 [\cf16 float_dtype\cf5 ,\cf16  layout\cf5 ,\cf16  MutAnyOrigin\cf5 ],\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     out_tensor\cf5 :\cf16  LayoutTensor\cf5 [\cf16 float_dtype\cf5 ,\cf16  layout\cf5 ,\cf16  MutAnyOrigin\cf5 ],\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 ):\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf23 """Calculate the element-wise sum of two vectors on the GPU."""\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf6 # Calculate the index of the vector element for the thread to process\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf16  tid \cf5 =\cf16  block_idx\cf5 .\cf16 x \cf5 *\cf16  block_dim\cf5 .\cf16 x \cf5 +\cf16  thread_idx\cf5 .\cf16 x\cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf6 # Don't process out of bounds elements\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 if\cf16  tid \cf5 <\cf16  vector_size\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         out_tensor\cf5 [\cf16 tid\cf5 ]\cf16  \cf5 =\cf16  lhs_tensor\cf5 [\cf16 tid\cf5 ]\cf16  \cf5 +\cf16  rhs_tensor\cf5 [\cf16 tid\cf5 ]\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Our 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 vector_addition()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  kernel function accepts the two input tensors and the output tensor as arguments. We also need to know the size of the vector (which we've defined with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 vector_size
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) because it might not be a multiple of the block size. In fact, in this example, the size of the vector is 1,000, which is not a multiple of our block size of 256. So as we assign our threads to read elements from the tensor, we need to make sure we don't overrun the bounds of the tensor.\
The body of the kernel function starts by calculating the linear index of the tensor element that a particular thread is responsible for. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 block_dim
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  object (which we added to the list of imports) contains the dimensions of the thread blocks as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 x
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 y
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 z
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values. Because we're going to use a one-dimensional grid of thread blocks, we need only the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 x
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  dimension. We can then calculate 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 tid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the unique "global" index of the thread within the output tensor, as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 block_dim.x * block_idx.x + thread_idx.x
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . For example, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 tid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values for the threads in the first thread block range from 0 to 255, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 tid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values for the threads in the second thread block range from 256 to 511, and so on.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 As a convenience, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cb20 \ul gpu}} package includes a 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 global_idx
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 comptime
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  value that contains the unique "global" 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 x
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 , 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 y
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 , and 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 z
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  indices of the thread within the grid of thread blocks. So for our one-dimensional grid of one-dimensional thread blocks, 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 global_idx.x
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  is equivalent to the value of 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 tid
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  that we calculated above. However, for this tutorial, it's best that you learn how to calculate 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 tid
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  manually so that you understand how the grid and thread block dimensions work.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The function then checks if the calculated 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 tid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is less than the size of the output tensor. If it is, the thread reads the corresponding elements from the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 lhs_tensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 rhs_tensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  tensors, adds them together, and stores the result in the corresponding element of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 out_tensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  tensor.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 10. Invoke the kernel function and copy the output back to the CPU\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The last step is to compile and invoke the kernel function, then copy the output back to the CPU. Add the following code to the end of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 main()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf16 \cb22 vector_addition.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15         \cf6 # Compile and enqueue the kernel\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         ctx\cf5 .\cf16 enqueue_function\cf5 [\cf16 vector_addition\cf5 ,\cf16  vector_addition\cf5 ](\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             lhs_tensor\cf5 ,\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             rhs_tensor\cf5 ,\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             result_tensor\cf5 ,\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             grid_dim\cf5 =\cf16 num_blocks\cf5 ,\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             block_dim\cf5 =\cf16 block_size\cf5 ,\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15         \cf6 # Create a HostBuffer for the result vector\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         result_host_buffer \cf5 =\cf16  ctx\cf5 .\cf16 enqueue_create_host_buffer\cf5 [\cf16 float_dtype\cf5 ](\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             vector_size\cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15         \cf6 # Copy the result vector from the DeviceBuffer to the HostBuffer\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         ctx\cf5 .\cf16 enqueue_copy\cf5 (\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             dst_buf\cf5 =\cf16 result_host_buffer\cf5 ,\cf16  src_buf\cf5 =\cf16 result_device_buffer\cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15         \cf6 # Finally, synchronize the DeviceContext to run all enqueued operations\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         ctx\cf5 .\cf16 synchronize\cf5 ()\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15         \cf5 print(\cf23 "Result vector:"\cf5 ,\cf16  result_host_buffer\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 Click here to see the complete version of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 vector_addition.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb4 .\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf10 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 enqueue_function()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method enqueues the compilation and invocation of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 vector_addition()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  kernel function, passing the input and output tensors as arguments. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 grid_dim
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 block_dim
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  arguments use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 num_blocks
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 block_size
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values we defined in the previous step.\
After the kernel function has been compiled and enqueued, we create a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 HostBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to hold the result vector. Then we copy the result vector from the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 DeviceBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 HostBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Finally, we synchronize the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 DeviceContext
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to run all enqueued operations. After synchronizing, we can print the result vector to the console.\
At this point, the Mojo compiler determines that the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 DeviceContext
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 DeviceBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 s, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 HostBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 s, and the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 LayoutTensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 s are no longer used and so it automatically invokes their destructors to free their allocated memory. (For a detailed explanation of object lifetime and destruction in Mojo, see the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/death/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Death of a value}} section of the Mojo Manual.)\
Now it's time to run the program to see the results of our work.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 mojo vector_addition.mojo\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
